{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>And he blessed Joseph and said.</b> He opened with the blessing of Joseph, but he blessed no one but the lads." "<b>And by way of</b> the plain sense (peshat): from here one may learn that the blessing of the lads is itself the blessing of Joseph, and the merit of the lads is all the merit of Joseph." "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah: in this blessing there is an allusion to the totality of the ten sefirot, for האלהים, the great and the mighty — they receive from above; and he called them Abraham and Isaac. For according to the plain sense the verse should have said 'the Elohim before whom my fathers Abraham and Isaac walked,' but instead it says 'the Elohim before whom my fathers walked, Abraham and Isaac'; and this is like (Genesis 35:7): ״כי שם נגלו אליו האלהים״. Afterward he said 'the Elohim who has been my shepherd' — this is אביר יעקב, and this is what is written (Psalms 80:2): ״רועה ישראל האזינה נוהג כצאן יוסף״. And the expression 'shepherd' (רועה) is from the expression of 'friend' (ריע), for in that attribute there is peace and lovingkindness. And afterward he sealed his words with the final mention, where he called him המלאך הגואל, and he said מקרב הארץ, in the manner of ״כי שמי בקרבו״. And you must know that had Jacob begun his prayer from the angel, this would have been cutting down the plantings; but he began with the word האלהים, which are the attributes that receive from the supernal, and he concluded with the angel, the redeemer, to seal his prayer with him, and then he unified all in a complete unification. And if you consider well, you will find that this blessing with which he blessed the lads here — it is like the blessing with which he blessed Joseph below, which is what he said (Genesis 49:24): ״מידי אביר יעקב משם רועה אבן ישראל״. For the expression ידי is the two hands of the throne of Solomon, the great and the mighty, corresponding to which he mentioned here Abraham and Isaac. אביר יעקב is תפארת ישראל. And you will not find in all the Torah the expression אביר אברהם or אביר יצחק, but only אביר יעקב; and this is what is written (Psalms 132:2): ״נדר לאביר יעקב״, and we say in prayer עננו אביר יעקב, and the intent is toward תפארת ישראל. And therefore you will not find this expression except with Jacob — אביר יעקב, or אביר ישראל, which is Jacob. Corresponding to it he mentioned here האלהים הרועה אותי, which is ״משם רועה אבן ישראל״ — its meaning is: from there, from the hand of אביר יעקב, which is תפארת ישראל, the strength and emanation of אבן ישראל — which is כנסת ישראל — is received; and it is the chief cornerstone, the sealing one; and the expression אבן, in the building, completes everything; corresponding to it he mentioned here המלאך הגואל אותי." "<b>And from this,</b> there is no reason to wonder that Jacob opened with the blessing of Joseph and blessed the lads, for the blessing of the lads is the blessing of Joseph, in the quality and inwardness of the matter of Joseph. And indeed the language of the verses fits this very well, and it testifies to it."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 48, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:21:17.500325600Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}